## 1. Introduction
Various industries were identified as emitters of ammonia. These include a fertilizer manufacture, coke manufacture, fossil fuel combustion, livestock management, and refrigeration equipment production. Ammonia contributes to several environmental problems, including direct toxic effects on vegetation and an atmospheric nitrogen deposition, which leads to the eutrophication and acidification of sensitive ecosystems, and to the formation of a secondary particulate matter in the atmosphere. The latter effects human health, atmospheric visibility and global radiative balance. To control the emissions of ammonia several adsorbents, such as powdered or granulated activated carbons [1,2], alumina [3], zeolites [4], and graphite oxide (GO) [5,6] have being investigated. The small size of ammonia $(3\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ [7] enables only weak retention forces on these materials. To overcome this problem, the adsorbents of ammonia must have a well-developed pore structure, with the pores similar in size to the size of the ammonia molecule, as well as the functional groups on the surface, to increase their reactivity with $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ . In this regard, materials called metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [8–11] have been tested as ammonia adsorbents [12–15]. The results obtained on the different types of MOFs showed both the importance of the nature of the metal sites and the functionality of the organic linkers in the retention process [15]. Although, these results are quite promising, the application of MOFs appeared to have some limitations for a small molecule like ammonia. These limitations refer to the completely open structure of MOF with a poor density of atoms [14]. To enhance the adsorption forces while preserving the beneficial features of MOF for ammonia retention, the nanocomposites of MOF with GO were prepared and tested [16–19]. Such nanocomposites showed an increase in the retention of ammonia mainly due to the enhanced dispersive forces in the unique small pores of the composites. These forces act at the interface between the MOF units and the graphene layers, and they account for a synergistic effect in ammonia adsorption compared to the parent materials [16–18]. The retention of ammonia on a zinc-based MOF was suggested to take place via hydrogen-bonding to the zinc oxide tetrahedra but led to a destruction of the MOF structure [16]. The retention of ammonia on a copper-based MOF occurs via its binding to the copper sites and then a progressive reaction with the MOF component [18].
The objective of this study is to investigate the MOF/GO nanocomposites as ammonia adsorbents at ambient conditions, and to emphasize the role of the specific surface groups of the composites in the adsorption process. Specifically in this study, the Brodie method [20] was used for the oxidation of different types of GO graphitic precursors, which differentiates the results addressed in this paper from our earlier studies of ammonia adsorption where
GO were oxidized using Hummers method [5]. The application of the Brodie method resulted in a different surface chemistry of GOs than that resulted from the usage of the Hummers method, which is expected to shed more light on the role of surface oxygen-containing groups in ammonia reactive adsorption. Owing to a various graphitic precursor, the synthesized GOs differ in the nature of oxygen-containing groups present on their surface [21]. Based on that and on the amount of GO used for the synthesis, the composites exhibit differences in their chemical and structural features [21]. These features have been addressed in details in the previous study [21], and some of them will be reintroduced in this paper for the clarity of discussion. They are expected to play an important role in the process of ammonia adsorption and their analysis will expand our understanding of the nature of active centers of the composites involved in the removal process of $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ .
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
The details on the synthesis of the parent materials, referred to as MOF and GO, were addressed elsewhere [20,21]. It was done following the method described by Millward and co-workers for MOF [22], and Brodie method for GO [20]. The preparation of the composites was done by adding GO powder to the well-dissolved copper nitrate hemipentahydrate and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid in N,N dimethylformamide (DMF). The resulting suspensions were sonicated for $5\mathrm{min}$ , stirred for another $30\mathrm{min}$ and then the same synthesis procedure as for MOF was carried out [22]. The added GO consisted of 10 and $30\mathrm{wt}.\%$ of the final material weight. Based on the graphite precursor, GO materials are designated as ‘‘GOB’’ and ‘‘GO1’’. The composites are referred as $n G O x\mathrm{{M}}$ , where $n$ is 10 or 30, and $x$ is B or 1.
## 2.2. $N H_{3}$ breakthrough dynamic test
In order to determine the ammonia breakthrough capacity, dynamic breakthrough tests were performed at room temperature. In a typical test, a flow of ammonia diluted with air went through a fixed bed of adsorbent with the total inlet flow rate of $225~\mathrm{mL}/\$ min and an ammonia concentration of $1000\mathrm{ppm}$ . The relative humidity of air in moist conditions was $70\%$ . The adsorbent’s bed contained about $2{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}$ of glass beads well mixed with the amount of adsorbent required to obtain a homogeneous bed. The mixture was packed into a glass column. The beads were used to avoid the pressure drop and thus to favor the kinetics of the breakthrough tests. The concentration of ammonia in the outlet gas was measured using an electrochemical sensor (Multi-Gas Monitor ITX system). The adsorption capacity of each adsorbent was calculated in mg per $\mathbf{g}$ of an adsorbent by integration of the area above the breakthrough curve. Tests in dry and wet conditions were implemented by diluting the ammonia stream with either dry or moist air, respectively. This was done to analyze the effects of water on the adsorption capacity. After the breakthrough tests, all samples were exposed to a flow of carrier air only $(180\mathrm{mL}/\$ min) to impose the desorption of ammonia and thus to evaluate the strength of its retention. The suffixes –ED and –EM are added to the name of the samples after exposure to ammonia in dry and moist conditions, respectively.
## 2.3. XRD
X-ray diffraction (XRD) measurements were carried out using a Philips X’Pert X-ray diffractometer. Adsorbents were ground with DMF or ethanol in a small agate mortar. The mixture was smear
mounted onto a glass slide and analyzed by Cu Ka radiation, with a voltage of $40\mathrm{kV}$ and current of $40\mathrm{mA}.$ .
## 2.4. FT-IR
FT-IR spectroscopy was carried out using a Nicolet MagnaIR830 spectrometer according to the attenuated total reflectance method (ATR). The experiments were done on the powdered samples, without KBr addition.
## 2.5. Thermal analysis
## 2.6. Nitrogen adsorption
Nitrogen adsorption isotherms were measured using an ASAP 2020 analyzer (Micromeritics, Norcross, GA, USA) at $-196^{\circ}C$ . Prior to the experiment, the samples were degassed at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to a constant pressure of $10^{-4}$ Torr. The surface area $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ (BET method), the total pore volume $V_{\mathrm{t}},$ the micropore volume $V_{\mathrm{mic}}$ (Dubinin-Radushkevich method) [23], and the mesopore volume $V_{\mathrm{mes}}$ were obtained from the isotherms.
## 3. Results and discussion
The ammonia breakthrough and desorption curves for the parent GOs and their composites with MOF, measured either in dry or in moist air, are presented in Fig. 1. For a better comparison, these curves were plotted in units of time per mass of an adsorbent, since the latter quantity varied from one material to another. The steep breakthrough and desorption curves, observed for most of the materials in dry conditions, suggest the fast kinetics of adsorption and the strong retention of ammonia on the adsorbents, respectively. On the other hand, the slower rise in breakthrough concentrations of ammonia, such as for 10GO1M and MOF in dry conditions, and for all the samples in moist conditions, suggests the complexity of the adsorption process. More so, a slow decrease in concentrations of ammonia for some exhausted materials upon purging with air, both in dry and moist conditions, indicates the weaker adsorption of ammonia on those samples.
From the breakthrough curves, the ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ breakthrough capacities of the materials were calculated, and the values are collected in Table 1. In dry air, the capacity of GO1 is seven times higher than that of GOB. Although, the adsorption of ammonia on GOs is rather linked to the surface chemistry and not to the porosity of these materials [5,24], the presence of some pores in GO1 compared to a completely non-porous structure of GOB [21] should be taken into consideration. Even though the porosities of these GOs, as well as the porosities of their composites with MOF, have been addressed in details elsewhere [21], the parameters of the porous structure are reintroduced in this paper for the clarity of the discussion (Table 2). The mechanism of ammonia adsorption on GOs, addressed in literature, suggests the intercalation of ammonia between the graphene layers and reactions with oxygen-containing groups [5,24]. The presence of these groups on GO1 (15.2 at.% of oxygen) [21] in addition to its developed porosity (Table 2) makes it a better adsorbent of ammonia than GOB, which does not have any carboxylic groups on its surface [21]. Furthermore, in moist conditions, the decrease in the adsorption capacity of
GO1 is related to the competition between ammonia and water for the adsorption sites on this highly hydrophilic material [5].
Comparison of the ammonia adsorption on the composites of MOF with GO1 and GOB, indicates a better performance of the former materials. However, it is important to mention that the ammonia adsorption capacities of both composites do not exceed those measured on MOF. The obtained trend is most likely the result of a decrease in the surface area and pore volumes (Table 2), as well as in the amount of active sites on MOF units, upon composites’ formation with GOs.
The measured adsorption capacities of the composite materials are also compared to the ‘‘hypothetical’’ ones. The latter ones are calculated assuming the physical mixture of GO and MOF, taking into account the percentage of each component in the composites and the adsorption capacity of GO and MOF alone [18] (Fig. 2). In general, the ‘‘hypothetical’’ capacities are higher than the measured ones, with the exception of 30GO1M, exposed to ammonia in moist conditions.
When the breakthrough tests were conducted in dry air, the $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ breakthrough capacities were higher for the composites with $10\%$ GO than those for the composites with $30\%$ GO, and 10GO1M was the best performing sample. The plausible explanation of this trend is that with an increase in the amount of GO in a composite more surface functional groups on GO are chemically involved in engaging the active sites of MOF-unsaturated copper centers. This process leaves less of these sites available for the interaction with ammonia. Thus, the lower ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ adsorption capacity of 10GOBM than that of 10GO1M, is due to the higher amount of epoxy groups on GOB, suggested as the ones involved in the composites’ formation when the GO content in the composite is small [21]. With the further increase in the GO content, the carboxylic groups located at the edges of the flakes of GO1 [21], react with copper, decreasing the number of active sites being able to bind ammonia, and thus lowering the ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ adsorption capacity of 30GO1M. As a further proof for this, the most significant decrease in the adsorption capacity compared to its ‘‘hypothetical’’ one is observed for this material in dry conditions (Fig. 2).
The dependence between the amount of GO in the composite and ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ adsorption capacity in dry air is shown in Fig. 3. The general trend shows a decrease in the amount of ammonia adsorbed with an increase in the percent of GO in a composite. This relationship appears to be not linear, suggesting that the amount adsorbed stops changing as all the active sites on MOF get involved with the oxygen groups on GOs.
The changes in porosities of the composites studied after ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ adsorption are seen in Table 2. Thus, the decrease in the total pore volumes of the composites after ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ adsorption in dry conditions, along with some increase in their mesopore volumes, is related to the breakage of the bonds between the copper centers and organic ligands, and thus partial collapse of the MOF structure.
When the breakthrough tests were performed in a moist air, the ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ breakthrough capacities of the composites were found to be higher than those obtained in the tests carried out in a dry air. It is also interesting that the increase in capacity is much more pronounced for the composites containing $30\%$ GO than for those containing $10\%$ GO (Table 1). Petit and coworkers [18] suggested that the enhanced porosity of the materials provides more space in the composites for water to be adsorbed, and consequently more ammonia can be retained on the surface via dissolution in a water film on the pore walls of the adsorbent. In our case, since the composites with $30\%$ GO exhibit lower porosities than their $10\%$ counterparts (Table 2), their higher ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ adsorption capacities should not be related to the dissolution of ammonia in water, but are rather linked to the specificity of surface chemistry. It was suggested in a different study that the collapse of the MOF structure in moist conditions can cause an increase in ammonia adsorption capacities of the composites studied [25]. Such an increase was explained to be the result of a release of carboxylic groups from the linkages of MOF, as well as some oxygen groups from GOs, which become the important centers for the acid–base reactions with ammonia [25]. Since the composites with $30\%$ GO have more of the oxygen groups, originating from GOs, more of these groups are released during the composite collapse, and become available for the reactions with ammonia. The reaction mechanisms of ammonia with epoxy, phenol and carboxylic groups of GOs were previously reported and the formation of amines and/or amides was found [5,6,24]. In our case, it is the presence of carboxylic groups on GO1, compared to the epoxy-rich GOB [21], and their subsequent release and dissociation that can explain the higher amount of ammonia adsorbed in moist conditions on 30GO1M than that on 30GOBM. The collapse of the MOF structure after the adsorption of ammonia in moist conditions is clearly visible from the significant decrease in the surface area and pore volumes of the composites (Table 2). This decrease is especially well-pronounced for the GO1 composites, which became practically nonporous after ammonia adsorption. It is likely, that water initially reacts with the MOF units, causing their decomposition, and release of the carboxylic groups, which then interact with ammonia.
It is noteworthy, that 30GO1M is the only sample, for which a significant improvement in the ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ breakthrough capacity in moist conditions, compared to the ‘‘hypothetical’’ one, was found (Fig. 2). This indicates the presence of a synergistic effect between MOF and GO1, which results in the improvement of the performance. Of particular importance could be the high volume of mesopores in 30GO1M (Table 2), formed by the involvement of carboxylic groups of GO with the MOF units [21]. These mesopores might enhance the amount of ammonia adsorbed via adsorption of water and ammonia dissolution in the water film [18]. The competition between water and ammonia for the adsorption sites of 30GO1M, results in the breakthrough of ammonia relatively early in the breakthrough experiment, followed by the prolonged state of quasi-equilibrium conditions (Fig. 1B).
To better understand the effect of the composites’ chemistry on $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ adsorption, exhausted samples were analyzed and the results are compared to those obtained on the initial materials [21]. The Xray diffraction was used to detect the changes in the composites’ structure caused by ammonia adsorption (Fig. 4). The shift of a single $d_{002}$ peak from $2\theta\approx11.4^{\circ}$ for the initial GOs [21], toward $2\theta\approx13.4^{\circ}$ for GOs after ammonia adsorption (Fig. 4A) is observed both in dry and moist conditions. This shift indicates the decrease in the interlayer distance of GOs related to the reaction of ammonia with the functional groups present in the interlayer space of GOs [18,24]. Such reactions likely lead to a release of a ring strain in graphene layers, which results in their more efficient stacking [24].
As described elsewhere, the X-ray diffraction patterns of all the composites before ammonia adsorption exhibit the same features, which closely resemble the pattern of a crystalline MOF structure [21]. The X-ray diffraction patterns of the composites after $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ adsorption, presented in Fig. 4B, show a significant decrease in the intensities of peaks characteristic of a MOF structure [21]. It is also visible that the crystalline structure of MOF in the composites is better preserved after ammonia adsorption in a dry air than in a moist air. These results are in agreement with the porosity data presented earlier (Table 2).
The changes in the surface chemistry of the materials caused by ammonia adsorption were also investigated by thermal analysis. The obtained DTG curves are presented in Fig. 5. The DTG curves for the initial materials, although discussed in the previous study [21], are also shown in Fig. 5C for the sake of comparison. For the materials after ammonia adsorption, the first peak, between 30 and $130^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , corresponds to the removal of ammonia physically adsorbed in small pores (in dry conditions) and to the removal of physically adsorbed water or weakly adsorbed ammonia/ammonia dissolved in water (in moist conditions) [18]. The second peak located between 130 and $270^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is assigned to the removal of strongly adsorbed ammonia (on the copper sites, via hydrogen bonding to the oxygen atoms of the carboxylate ligands or from $(\mathsf{N H}_{4})_{3}\mathsf{B T C})$ [18,26] and/or ammonium copper complexes, formed by interaction of ammonia with copper salts [21]. The broad peak between 270 and $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ represents the decomposition of benzene tricarboxylate (BTC) on MOF, where shoulders revealed at this temperature range correspond to a variety of compounds containing BTC [18].
The DTG curves for 10GO1M and 10GOBM in dry conditions (Fig. 5B) show the complete disappearance of the peak around $290^{\circ}\mathsf C$ detected on these materials before the ammonia adsorption (Fig. 5C), and assigned to the decomposition of copper–carboxylic acid compounds/salts [19]. This indicates that such compounds do get involved in the ammonia adsorption. As a result, the peak between 130 and $270^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , especially well-pronounced for 10GO1M-ED, could be assigned to the ammonium copper complexes or ammonium salts of carboxylic acids [5].
In moist conditions, the complexity of the peak between 270 and $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ on the DTG curves of the composites with $30\%$ GO (Fig. 5A) once again suggests the participation of the MOF component in the ammonia adsorption and the formation of such compounds as $\mathrm{Cu(NH_{3})_{2}B T C_{2/3}}$ and $(\mathsf{N H}_{4})_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ [18]. These compounds appear as a result of ammonia adsorption on the copper sites or carboxylate sites of MOF, respectively. A significant weight loss between 30 and $130^{\circ}\mathsf C$ on the DTG curve of 30GO1M-EM corresponds to the removal of weakly adsorbed ammonia. This is in agreement with the result of the breakthrough test for this sample. The synergy between the two components resulted in the developed mesopores, where some of the ammonia was adsorbed via its dissolution in a water film inside those pores. Finally, it has to be mentioned, that the intensity of the peak at about $230{-}240^{\circ}C$ (Fig. 5C), corresponding to the decomposition of epoxy groups on GOs, decreases after ammonia adsorption. This supports the participation of these groups in the reactive adsorption process [21]. The effect is especially pronounced for the $30\%$ GO composites, where the amount of these groups is higher than in their $10\%$ counterparts.
The qualitative changes in the surface chemistry of the materials caused by the ammonia adsorption are further analyzed by FTIR (Fig. 6). As in the case of DTG data, for the sake of comparison, the spectra of the exhausted samples are presented along with the spectra of the initial samples [19]. The main changes are seen in the appearance of the new bands at ${\sim}1620$ , 1260 and $1240\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , in the increase in the intensity of the band at $\sim1560\ c m^{-1}$ , and in the appearance of the bands at 3340 and $3220\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The same bands were noticed by Petit and coworkers [18], when studying the adsorption of ammonia on the composites of MOF and GO obtained by Hummers method. The appearance of the first three bands is related to the spectra of BTC that is not coordinated to copper [27]. These bands are more pronounced for the materials exposed to ammonia in moist conditions. This confirms the distortion of copper-BTC bridging caused by the presence of moisture. The increase in the intensity of the $1560\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ band along with the appearance of the $1620\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , 3340 and $3220\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ bands further supports the participation of the carboxylic groups from BTC or copper–carboxylic acid compounds in the reactive adsorption of ammonia. These bands are associated with the ${\sf N\mathrm{-}}{\sf H}$ stretching, and are related to the vibrations of amines/amides [28,29]. The band at $1620\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ may also be related to vibrations of ammonia [5,23,29], and the bands at $1440\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3030{-}3300\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ – to $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ vibration in $\mathsf{N H}_{4}^{+}$ [28] either in ammonium salts or $(\mathsf{N H}_{4})_{3}\mathsf{B T C}.$ formed by ammonia interaction with carboxylic/epoxy groups of GOs and the carboxylic groups of BTC, respectively.
## 4. Conclusion
The results obtained showed that the amount of active sites on MOF in a composite is a crucial feature governing ammonia adsorption in dry conditions. With an increase in the amount of GO in a composite, more surface functional groups on GO are involved with the active sites on MOF, which leads to a decrease in the amount of ammonia adsorbed. In the presence of moisture, the adsorption capacities of the composites were found to be higher than those in dry conditions. The collapse of a MOF structure caused by water, results in a release of the active sites, such as the carboxylic groups from the linkages of MOF and oxygen groups from GOs, which are able to interact with ammonia. The higher amount of GO in a composite as well as the presence of carboxylic groups in a specific GO, results in a higher amount of these groups being released and dissociated, and thus being able to interact with ammonia. Among the products of ammonia adsorption on the composite materials, amines and amides were found, along with ammonium salts and/or copper ammonium complexes, formed as a result of ammonia reactions with the carboxylic groups of MOF and copper salts, respectively.
